1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:05,580
In May 1940 France had more
tanks than the Wehrmacht,

2
00:00:05,600 --> 00:00:10,290
the strongest fortified line in Europe and
one of the largest armies in the world.

3
00:00:10,440 --> 00:00:14,090
Six weeks later it had
to sign an armistice,

4
00:00:14,120 --> 00:00:16,450
and it did so in the very
same railway carriage

5
00:00:16,560 --> 00:00:20,770
in which Germany had ended
the First World War in 1918.

6
00:00:20,960 --> 00:00:25,000
The reason was not the
number of tanks, but a plan

7
00:00:25,020 --> 00:00:27,630
that almost nobody had thought possible.

8
00:00:29,180 --> 00:00:31,790
After the invasion of
Poland, Great Britain

9
00:00:31,810 --> 00:00:34,590
and France had declared war on Germany.

10
00:00:34,610 --> 00:00:37,040
But in the West there
was hardly any fighting.

11
00:00:37,130 --> 00:00:42,270
The French called these months the
"drôle de guerre", the strange war,

12
00:00:42,290 --> 00:00:47,290
the British the "Phoney War", and in
Germany people spoke of the Sitzkrieg.

13
00:00:47,310 --> 00:00:51,330
French soldiers waited in the
bunkers of the Maginot Line,

14
00:00:51,350 --> 00:00:54,440
a chain of fortresses
along the German border.

15
00:00:54,530 --> 00:00:57,420
Facing them, German
troops held the Westwall.

16
00:00:57,530 --> 00:01:00,830
British bombers dropped
no bombs over Germany,

17
00:01:00,850 --> 00:01:03,040
but millions of leaflets.

18
00:01:03,150 --> 00:01:06,260
A British Expeditionary
Force moved to France,

19
00:01:06,330 --> 00:01:09,980
and the Allies prepared for a long war.

20
00:01:10,150 --> 00:01:13,020
They believed time was on their side.

21
00:01:14,570 --> 00:01:16,120
Hitler saw it differently.

22
00:01:16,210 --> 00:01:22,260
As early as October 1939 he ordered an
attack in the West as soon as possible.

23
00:01:22,390 --> 00:01:25,800
His generals did not yet
consider the army ready,

24
00:01:25,890 --> 00:01:30,220
and the date was postponed again and
again, mostly because of the weather.

25
00:01:30,290 --> 00:01:33,730
The first plan called for
a thrust through Belgium,

26
00:01:33,750 --> 00:01:39,260
much like 1914. This was exactly
the attack the French expected.

27
00:01:39,350 --> 00:01:42,870
On 10 January 1940 a German courier plane

28
00:01:42,890 --> 00:01:46,340
lost its way in fog and had to make
an emergency landing in Belgium.

29
00:01:46,360 --> 00:01:51,480
On board was Major Hellmuth Reinberger
with parts of the attack plans.

30
00:01:51,600 --> 00:01:53,630
He tried to burn the papers,

31
00:01:53,650 --> 00:01:56,300
but Belgian soldiers secured them.

32
00:01:56,450 --> 00:02:01,260
Historians still disagree whether
this incident changed the German plan.

33
00:02:02,810 --> 00:02:06,240
What is certain is that
another draft already existed.

34
00:02:06,410 --> 00:02:11,820
It came from Lieutenant General Erich von
Manstein, chief of staff of Army Group A.

35
00:02:11,910 --> 00:02:15,320
Manstein did not want to make
the main thrust through Belgium,

36
00:02:15,450 --> 00:02:17,860
but further south through the Ardennes,

37
00:02:17,880 --> 00:02:19,670
a wooded upland

38
00:02:19,690 --> 00:02:22,790
that the French considered
almost impassable for tanks.

39
00:02:22,810 --> 00:02:28,220
The army command rejected the plan
and moved Manstein to a corps command.

40
00:02:28,350 --> 00:02:33,800
On 17 February 1940 he was still able
to present it to Hitler in person.

41
00:02:34,050 --> 00:02:35,940
Hitler adopted the idea.

42
00:02:37,490 --> 00:02:40,510
But first Germany struck in the north.

43
00:02:40,530 --> 00:02:44,330
In winter, the Swedish iron ore

44
00:02:44,350 --> 00:02:46,160
that German armament needed was
shipped through Norwegian ports.

45
00:02:46,230 --> 00:02:49,940
Britain planned to mine Norwegian waters.

46
00:02:49,970 --> 00:02:53,630
On 9 April 1940 Germany got there first.

47
00:02:53,650 --> 00:02:56,080
Operation Weserübung began.

48
00:02:56,190 --> 00:03:01,060
Denmark was occupied within a few
hours and surrendered the same day.

49
00:03:01,110 --> 00:03:05,110
In Norway German troops landed
in several ports at once,

50
00:03:05,130 --> 00:03:06,900
from Oslo to Narvik.

51
00:03:08,450 --> 00:03:13,140
In the Oslofjord the heavy cruiser Blücher
sailed towards the capital at night.

52
00:03:13,160 --> 00:03:17,170
At Oscarsborg Fortress
Norwegian guns opened fire,

53
00:03:17,190 --> 00:03:20,180
torpedoes hit the ship,
and the Blücher sank.

54
00:03:20,250 --> 00:03:22,220
Hundreds of men were killed.

55
00:03:22,360 --> 00:03:24,240
The delay was enough:

56
00:03:24,310 --> 00:03:29,570
King Haakon VII, the government and
Norway's gold reserves left Oslo.

57
00:03:29,590 --> 00:03:34,080
The King refused to recognise a
government installed by Germany.

58
00:03:34,100 --> 00:03:38,730
At Narvik the Kriegsmarine lost all
ten destroyers in two naval battles,

59
00:03:38,750 --> 00:03:40,200
that it had sent there.

60
00:03:40,220 --> 00:03:44,430
British, French and Polish
troops landed in Norway,

61
00:03:44,450 --> 00:03:46,970
but the fighting dragged on into June.

62
00:03:46,990 --> 00:03:49,640
Then the Allies had to
withdraw their troops,

63
00:03:49,690 --> 00:03:52,940
because by now everything
was at stake in France.

64
00:03:54,490 --> 00:03:59,160
On the morning of 10 May 1940 the
Wehrmacht attacked the Netherlands,

65
00:03:59,180 --> 00:04:00,950
Belgium and Luxembourg.

66
00:04:00,970 --> 00:04:05,030
On the same day Prime Minister Neville
Chamberlain resigned in London.

67
00:04:05,050 --> 00:04:07,560
His successor was Winston Churchill.

68
00:04:07,620 --> 00:04:10,450
Paratroopers jumped over the Netherlands

69
00:04:10,470 --> 00:04:12,650
to seize bridges and airfields.

70
00:04:12,670 --> 00:04:16,160
In Belgium gliders landed
fewer than a hundred

71
00:04:16,180 --> 00:04:19,930
airborne engineers directly on
the roof of Fort Eben-Emael,

72
00:04:19,950 --> 00:04:22,370
which was considered one
of the strongest in Europe.

73
00:04:22,390 --> 00:04:25,510
With shaped charges they
knocked out the guns.

74
00:04:25,530 --> 00:04:29,800
The next day around 780
Belgian soldiers surrendered.

75
00:04:29,930 --> 00:04:34,350
The attacks in the north looked
exactly as the Allies had expected.

76
00:04:34,370 --> 00:04:38,080
Their best formations, including
the British Expeditionary Force,

77
00:04:38,100 --> 00:04:41,750
advanced into Belgium to meet the enemy.

78
00:04:41,770 --> 00:04:43,880
With that, they walked into the trap.

79
00:04:45,430 --> 00:04:49,580
On 14 May, while the surrender
of the city was being negotiated,

80
00:04:49,600 --> 00:04:52,780
the Luftwaffe bombed
the centre of Rotterdam.

81
00:04:52,930 --> 00:04:58,080
Around 850 people died, almost
80,000 were left homeless.

82
00:04:58,330 --> 00:05:02,300
That same evening the Dutch
army laid down its arms.

83
00:05:02,410 --> 00:05:06,360
Queen Wilhelmina and the government
had already gone to London.

84
00:05:07,910 --> 00:05:12,620
At the same time, further south, the panzer
divisions pushed through the Ardennes.

85
00:05:12,730 --> 00:05:16,230
Tens of thousands of vehicles
rolled along narrow forest roads,

86
00:05:16,250 --> 00:05:18,760
and there were traffic
jams many kilometres long.

87
00:05:18,810 --> 00:05:21,670
Allied air reconnaissance
reported columns,

88
00:05:21,690 --> 00:05:25,660
but the French command did not
recognise where the main thrust lay.

89
00:05:25,870 --> 00:05:28,730
On 13 May the tanks of the group

90
00:05:28,750 --> 00:05:32,380
under Lieutenant General Heinz
Guderian reached the Meuse at Sedan.

91
00:05:32,480 --> 00:05:35,910
For hours the Luftwaffe
attacked the French positions,

92
00:05:35,930 --> 00:05:40,290
then infantry and engineers
crossed the river in rubber boats.

93
00:05:40,310 --> 00:05:42,500
The French front broke open.

94
00:05:44,050 --> 00:05:47,280
Many German soldiers
barely slept in those days.

95
00:05:47,490 --> 00:05:52,110
Between April and July
1940 more than 35 million

96
00:05:52,130 --> 00:05:56,730
tablets of Pervitin and Isophan were
delivered to the army and the Luftwaffe,

97
00:05:56,750 --> 00:06:00,260
a stimulant based on methamphetamine.

98
00:06:00,460 --> 00:06:05,520
The tanks now drove not towards Paris
but west, to the English Channel.

99
00:06:05,550 --> 00:06:10,610
One of the fastest was the 7th Panzer
Division under Major General Erwin Rommel.

100
00:06:10,630 --> 00:06:13,130
Because often even its
own command did not know

101
00:06:13,150 --> 00:06:17,260
where it was, it soon became
known as the Ghost Division.

102
00:06:17,360 --> 00:06:22,110
On 20 May the 2nd Panzer Division
reached the coast at Abbeville.

103
00:06:22,130 --> 00:06:25,010
The Allied armies in
Belgium and northern France

104
00:06:25,030 --> 00:06:27,440
were cut off from the rest of France.

105
00:06:27,640 --> 00:06:30,930
On 15 May the French prime minister

106
00:06:30,950 --> 00:06:33,700
Paul Reynaud had told
Churchill on the telephone:

107
00:06:33,770 --> 00:06:34,900
"We are beaten."

108
00:06:36,450 --> 00:06:40,830
The trapped troops fell back
to the coast, to Dunkirk.

109
00:06:40,940 --> 00:06:44,900
On 24 May the German tanks
stopped outside the city.

110
00:06:44,920 --> 00:06:49,820
The order came from Colonel General Gerd
von Rundstedt, and Hitler confirmed it.

111
00:06:49,840 --> 00:06:53,030
The Luftwaffe was to destroy
the pocket on its own.

112
00:06:53,280 --> 00:06:56,590
Only two days later were the
tanks allowed to move on.

113
00:06:56,680 --> 00:07:01,330
Why this halt order was given
still occupies historians today.

114
00:07:02,880 --> 00:07:06,620
On 26 May Operation Dynamo began.

115
00:07:06,640 --> 00:07:08,310
Destroyers, ferries,

116
00:07:08,340 --> 00:07:11,380
fishing boats and hundreds of
civilian vessels crossed the

117
00:07:11,400 --> 00:07:15,250
Channel and picked up soldiers
from the beaches and the mole.

118
00:07:15,360 --> 00:07:18,890
On 28 May the Belgian army capitulated.

119
00:07:18,910 --> 00:07:22,530
French troops defended
the city to the last.

120
00:07:22,560 --> 00:07:29,280
By 4 June 338,226 soldiers
had been brought to England,

121
00:07:29,300 --> 00:07:31,830
among them more than 100,000 Frenchmen.

122
00:07:31,900 --> 00:07:35,670
Almost all their heavy equipment
was left behind on the beach.

123
00:07:35,920 --> 00:07:40,790
Churchill told the House of Commons
that wars are not won by evacuations.

124
00:07:40,910 --> 00:07:45,370
But the soldiers Britain would
need later had been saved.

125
00:07:45,550 --> 00:07:50,170
And at Bletchley Park, north of
London, at the end of May 1940

126
00:07:50,450 --> 00:07:54,030
the Luftwaffe's radio messages
began to be decrypted regularly.

127
00:07:54,080 --> 00:07:57,450
The work of the Polish
mathematicians paid off.

128
00:07:59,000 --> 00:08:03,370
On 5 June the second phase of the
offensive began, towards the south.

129
00:08:03,440 --> 00:08:08,210
The French army was weakened, but
in many places it fought stubbornly.

130
00:08:08,380 --> 00:08:13,230
On 10 June Italy declared war
on France and Great Britain.

131
00:08:13,270 --> 00:08:18,220
Millions of French people fled south
ahead of the front, the roads were jammed.

132
00:08:18,240 --> 00:08:20,720
Paris was declared an open city,

133
00:08:20,740 --> 00:08:23,910
and on 14 June German troops marched in.

134
00:08:24,040 --> 00:08:27,790
The garrisons of the Maginot
Line were surrounded from behind.

135
00:08:27,860 --> 00:08:30,360
The government withdrew to Bordeaux.

136
00:08:30,380 --> 00:08:31,650
Reynaud resigned.

137
00:08:31,800 --> 00:08:35,880
His successor, the 84-year-old
Marshal Philippe Pétain,

138
00:08:35,900 --> 00:08:40,430
a hero of the First World War,
asked for an armistice on 17 June.

139
00:08:40,620 --> 00:08:45,500
The next day a little-known general
spoke on the BBC from London.

140
00:08:45,520 --> 00:08:49,430
Charles de Gaulle called on the
French to continue the fight.

141
00:08:50,980 --> 00:08:53,980
Hitler chose the place of signing himself.

142
00:08:54,000 --> 00:08:54,960
The saloon carriage

143
00:08:54,980 --> 00:08:58,140
in which the armistice
of the First World War

144
00:08:58,160 --> 00:09:00,820
had been signed in November 1918

145
00:09:00,840 --> 00:09:04,970
was brought out of its museum on his orders
and placed in the clearing at Compiègne.

146
00:09:05,140 --> 00:09:09,350
He sat in the seat Marshal
Foch had occupied back then.

147
00:09:09,490 --> 00:09:14,610
On 22 June 1940 the
French delegation signed.

148
00:09:14,800 --> 00:09:16,560
France was divided:

149
00:09:16,580 --> 00:09:19,350
the north and the Atlantic
coast were occupied,

150
00:09:19,400 --> 00:09:22,340
in the south Pétain governed from Vichy.

151
00:09:22,360 --> 00:09:26,430
Almost two million French
soldiers were taken prisoner,

152
00:09:26,480 --> 00:09:29,500
most of them spent years in German camps.

153
00:09:29,520 --> 00:09:33,770
In six weeks the Wehrmacht
lost around 27,000 dead.

154
00:09:33,840 --> 00:09:36,970
The carriage was taken to
Berlin and put on display.

155
00:09:38,520 --> 00:09:44,190
Early on the morning of 23 June Hitler
drove through empty Paris for a few hours,

156
00:09:44,310 --> 00:09:47,630
to the Opera, the Eiffel
Tower and Napoleon's tomb.

157
00:09:48,010 --> 00:09:51,570
In Germany the regime had the
church bells rung for a week.

158
00:09:51,960 --> 00:09:54,540
In just six weeks the
Wehrmacht had achieved

159
00:09:54,560 --> 00:09:57,910
what it had failed to do in four
years in the First World War.

160
00:09:58,010 --> 00:09:59,750
But one enemy remained.

161
00:09:59,840 --> 00:10:02,950
Britain rejected every peace offer.

162
00:10:03,100 --> 00:10:06,510
The army could not attack
across the English Channel.

163
00:10:06,530 --> 00:10:09,470
Now the Luftwaffe was to clear the way.
